1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,097
Maya: Satellites seeing in the dark now apparently.

2
00:00:04,097 --> 00:00:07,920
Maya: That, and five more stories, on The Brief.

3
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:10,748
Alex: This is Bits and Tickers.

4
00:00:10,748 --> 00:00:13,265
Alex: What got built, and what it's worth.

5
00:00:13,265 --> 00:00:13,894
Alex: I'm Alex.

6
00:00:14,710 --> 00:00:15,610
Maya: And I'm Maya.

7
00:00:15,610 --> 00:00:20,943
Maya: It's Thursday, November 12th, and this is The Brief: the stories that matter,

8
00:00:20,943 --> 00:00:22,120
Maya: in a few minutes.

9
00:00:22,120 --> 00:00:25,791
Maya: Everything we talk about is linked in the show notes.

10
00:00:26,606 --> 00:00:27,721
Alex: On today's show.

11
00:00:27,721 --> 00:00:30,856
Alex: Satellites seeing in the dark now apparently.

12
00:00:30,856 --> 00:00:33,295
Alex: Chips meet AI in a surprising deal.

13
00:00:33,295 --> 00:00:36,360
Alex: Founders cashing out big on Anthropic's IPO.

14
00:00:36,360 --> 00:00:39,704
Alex: ChatGPT is letting businesses build AI chatbots.

15
00:00:39,704 --> 00:00:42,143
Alex: Big shakeup at Meta's data centers.

16
00:00:42,143 --> 00:00:44,930
Alex: And CoreWeave's stock just jumped a lot.

17
00:00:44,930 --> 00:00:46,184
Alex: Let's get into it.

18
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:48,729
Maya: Time for The Ticker.

19
00:00:48,729 --> 00:00:54,607
Maya: At Wednesday's close, the S&P 500 finished at 7,619, up 1.3 percent.

20
00:00:54,607 --> 00:01:00,744
Maya: The Nasdaq Composite was up 1.6 percent and the Dow was up 0.5 percent.

21
00:01:01,768 --> 00:01:06,617
Alex: And in big tech, Tesla had the best day, up 2.9 percent,

22
00:01:06,617 --> 00:01:11,379
Alex: while Microsoft was at the other end, down 2.3 percent.

23
00:01:11,379 --> 00:01:13,976
Alex: That's a snapshot, not advice.

24
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:20,035
Alex: Anduril is claiming they’ve demonstrated a satellite that can track spacecraft even

25
00:01:20,035 --> 00:01:21,916
Alex: when they’re in Earth’s shadow.

26
00:01:21,916 --> 00:01:23,554
Alex: Ars Technica has the story.

27
00:01:24,272 --> 00:01:25,073
Maya: Wait, really?

28
00:01:25,073 --> 00:01:26,306
Maya: Tracking spacecraft?

29
00:01:27,024 --> 00:01:28,053
Alex: Yeah, apparently.

30
00:01:28,053 --> 00:01:32,168
Alex: It's their satellite, Anduril-216, orbiting about 590 kilometers up.

31
00:01:32,886 --> 00:01:36,595
Maya: That’s a lot of hardware for what sounds like a niche problem.

32
00:01:37,313 --> 00:01:39,489
Alex: It's a tech demo, funded internally.

33
00:01:39,489 --> 00:01:43,115
Alex: They launched it on a SpaceX rideshare mission back in July.

34
00:01:43,833 --> 00:01:46,106
Maya: And what exactly is it doing up there?

35
00:01:46,824 --> 00:01:51,743
Alex: It's got an infrared imager that works during those dark periods—about a third of

36
00:01:51,743 --> 00:01:52,411
Alex: each orbit.

37
00:01:52,411 --> 00:01:54,780
Alex: They’re using it to spot other objects.

38
00:01:55,498 --> 00:02:00,267
Maya: So, just an edge-compute processor, power management, and this infrared sensor.

39
00:02:00,267 --> 00:02:02,018
Maya: Seems like a focused payload.

40
00:02:02,736 --> 00:02:03,221
Alex: Exactly.

41
00:02:03,221 --> 00:02:07,282
Alex: They’re saying it can see maneuvers others might be trying to hide.

42
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:10,246
Maya: So, TSMC, the biggest chipmaker around,

43
00:02:10,246 --> 00:02:13,413
Maya: is putting a billion dollars into OpenAI and Anthropic.

44
00:02:13,413 --> 00:02:15,543
Maya: That’s the surprising part, isn't it?

45
00:02:16,224 --> 00:02:19,967
Alex: I thought they were just happily selling chips to those companies.

46
00:02:20,648 --> 00:02:24,759
Maya: They’ve already made a ton of money supplying chips for AI data centers.

47
00:02:24,759 --> 00:02:29,099
Maya: This investment is up to 500 million in OpenAI and another 500 in Anthropic.

48
00:02:29,779 --> 00:02:30,524
Alex: That's a lot.

49
00:02:30,524 --> 00:02:35,054
Alex: What's the breakdown – preferred stock for OpenAI, common shares for Anthropic?

50
00:02:35,734 --> 00:02:35,965
Maya: Yep.

51
00:02:35,965 --> 00:02:38,156
Maya: Apparently, the board approved it all.

52
00:02:38,156 --> 00:02:42,654
Maya: It's a bit of a move to give the AI market a boost, according to Ars Technica.

53
00:02:43,334 --> 00:02:45,853
Alex: I wonder how much say they’ll actually have.

54
00:02:45,853 --> 00:02:49,403
Alex: Are they just throwing money or expecting something in return?

55
00:02:50,084 --> 00:02:54,821
Maya: Analysts are concerned it makes it harder to see what’s actually driving demand for

56
00:02:54,821 --> 00:02:59,045
Maya: AI hardware and services, you know, with this kind of circular investment.

57
00:02:59,725 --> 00:03:00,820
Alex: Circular financing…

58
00:03:00,820 --> 00:03:03,299
Alex: yeah, I can see why that's tricky to value.

59
00:03:03,979 --> 00:03:04,319
Maya: It is.

60
00:03:05,000 --> 00:03:08,941
Alex: Maya, did you see this TechCrunch piece about Anthropic’s IPO?

61
00:03:08,941 --> 00:03:13,200
Alex: Seems like the founders are all taking a pretty significant payday.

62
00:03:13,957 --> 00:03:15,849
Maya: $250 million each, apparently.

63
00:03:16,606 --> 00:03:19,788
Alex: Yeah, each selling just over three million shares.

64
00:03:19,788 --> 00:03:23,670
Alex: It’s interesting they’re all selling the same amount, though.

65
00:03:24,427 --> 00:03:27,163
Maya: It does suggest some level of coordination.

66
00:03:27,163 --> 00:03:31,618
Maya: They’ll still own about 3.2% of Class A shares each, collectively 22%.

67
00:03:32,375 --> 00:03:33,951
Alex: They’re still in control?

68
00:03:34,708 --> 00:03:38,334
Maya: They’ll have 51.1% of the votes, thanks to Class F stock.

69
00:03:38,334 --> 00:03:42,214
Maya: Amazon and Google also have shares, but of a different class.

70
00:03:42,971 --> 00:03:47,576
Alex: So, they left OpenAI to focus on AI safety, and now they’re getting rich.

71
00:03:48,332 --> 00:03:49,743
Maya: That’s the story, yes.

72
00:03:49,743 --> 00:03:52,243
Maya: Reed Hastings wants in too, apparently.

73
00:03:53,000 --> 00:03:57,176
Maya: OpenAI's letting businesses create AI agents to represent them within ChatGPT.

74
00:03:57,819 --> 00:03:59,157
Alex: Like, full-on brand reps?

75
00:03:59,800 --> 00:04:00,400
Maya: Apparently.

76
00:04:00,400 --> 00:04:04,492
Maya: The Verge reports it’s starting as a beta program for e-commerce and online

77
00:04:04,492 --> 00:04:05,529
Maya: services in the US.

78
00:04:06,172 --> 00:04:07,617
Alex: That's a lot of businesses.

79
00:04:08,260 --> 00:04:11,771
Maya: Businesses can specify instructions and context for these agents,

80
00:04:11,771 --> 00:04:14,471
Maya: so they can answer questions about their products.

81
00:04:15,113 --> 00:04:16,987
Alex: So, comparison shopping, basically?

82
00:04:17,630 --> 00:04:20,385
Maya: That’s the main use case they're highlighting, yes.

83
00:04:20,385 --> 00:04:23,627
Maya: And there's this thing called Business Hub for verification.

84
00:04:24,269 --> 00:04:24,983
Alex: Verification?

85
00:04:24,983 --> 00:04:26,357
Alex: How do they even do that?

86
00:04:27,000 --> 00:04:31,083
Alex: This one caught my eye because it’s a pretty significant shift in Meta’s

87
00:04:31,083 --> 00:04:35,392
Alex: infrastructure leadership – their head of infrastructure, Santosh Janardhan,

88
00:04:35,392 --> 00:04:36,016
Alex: is leaving.

89
00:04:36,688 --> 00:04:37,150
Maya: Leaving?

90
00:04:37,150 --> 00:04:37,727
Maya: Seriously?

91
00:04:37,727 --> 00:04:40,496
Maya: That’s a lot of experience walking out the door.

92
00:04:41,168 --> 00:04:44,227
Alex: Apparently he’s handing over the reins to David Brown,

93
00:04:44,227 --> 00:04:46,096
Alex: who just joined from AWS in July.

94
00:04:46,768 --> 00:04:49,380
Maya: Hold on, so he’s only been there a few months?

95
00:04:49,380 --> 00:04:50,856
Maya: That’s a quick transition.

96
00:04:51,528 --> 00:04:55,449
Alex: Yeah, Janardhan will support Brown through the first quarter of 2027,

97
00:04:55,449 --> 00:04:56,813
Alex: so it's not cold turkey.

98
00:04:56,813 --> 00:04:59,256
Alex: CNBC reports he said it's “the right time.”

99
00:04:59,928 --> 00:05:00,271
Maya: Right.

100
00:05:00,271 --> 00:05:04,843
Maya: And this is all part of Mark Zuckerberg’s push to expand data center operations,

101
00:05:04,843 --> 00:05:05,472
Maya: apparently.

102
00:05:06,144 --> 00:05:10,400
Alex: Seems like it’s linked to investor pressure regarding capital spending, too.

103
00:05:11,072 --> 00:05:14,208
Maya: Which, given their negative free cash flow, makes sense.

104
00:05:14,880 --> 00:05:15,328
Alex: Exactly.

105
00:05:16,000 --> 00:05:19,894
Maya: Apparently Jim Cramer is warming up to CoreWeave, according to CNBC.

106
00:05:19,894 --> 00:05:24,532
Maya: Their stock surged nearly 25% after they released earnings and the CEO had a chat

107
00:05:24,532 --> 00:05:25,047
Maya: with him.

108
00:05:25,725 --> 00:05:26,131
Alex: Cramer?

109
00:05:26,131 --> 00:05:28,043
Alex: I thought he’d be more skeptical.

110
00:05:28,722 --> 00:05:31,015
Maya: Yeah, he was, but he’s changed his tune.

111
00:05:31,015 --> 00:05:34,512
Maya: They’re seeing demand ten times greater than they can supply,

112
00:05:34,512 --> 00:05:36,920
Maya: which is allowing them to increase prices.

113
00:05:37,599 --> 00:05:40,369
Alex: Makes you wonder how sustainable that is, though.

114
00:05:41,048 --> 00:05:43,899
Maya: They’re also dealing with a significant debt load,

115
00:05:43,899 --> 00:05:47,720
Maya: which has been weighing down the stock, but the surge helped a bit.

116
00:05:48,398 --> 00:05:51,834
Alex: So, they're financing with equipment and contracts now, huh?

117
00:05:51,834 --> 00:05:52,865
Alex: Interesting pivot.

118
00:05:53,543 --> 00:05:57,986
Maya: Apparently the CEO was tempted to buy back their own bonds on the open market,

119
00:05:57,986 --> 00:06:01,289
Maya: but he figures building data centers is the better return.

120
00:06:01,968 --> 00:06:03,171
Alex: And the Nvidia chips?

121
00:06:03,171 --> 00:06:06,322
Alex: They're holding their value longer than people thought.

122
00:06:07,000 --> 00:06:07,999
Alex: That's the news.

123
00:06:07,999 --> 00:06:09,372
Alex: Time for The Takeaway.

124
00:06:10,102 --> 00:06:15,009
Alex: I just think the satellite stuff—seeing maneuvers in orbit—sounds like a massive

125
00:06:15,009 --> 00:06:17,646
Alex: engineering challenge, even if it’s a demo.

126
00:06:18,376 --> 00:06:23,028
Maya: I’m going to be watching how quickly those Business Agents for ChatGPT start

127
00:06:23,028 --> 00:06:28,109
Maya: impacting actual productivity and, crucially, the bottom line for those businesses.

128
00:06:28,839 --> 00:06:33,379
Alex: I'm curious to see if Meta's infrastructure shift really changes anything,

129
00:06:33,379 --> 00:06:35,895
Alex: or if it's just a shuffle of deck chairs.

130
00:06:36,625 --> 00:06:41,534
Maya: Alex, you’re so dismissive of Cramer—I think he's genuinely on to something with

131
00:06:41,534 --> 00:06:43,682
Maya: CoreWeave, even if it's a bit loud.

132
00:06:44,412 --> 00:06:46,602
Alex: Hold on, loud doesn't equal correct.

133
00:06:47,332 --> 00:06:48,626
Maya: And that's The Brief.

134
00:06:48,626 --> 00:06:52,077
Maya: Every story we talked about is linked in the show notes.

135
00:06:52,807 --> 00:06:55,048
Alex: Next up is The Long Drive, tomorrow.

136
00:06:55,048 --> 00:06:57,974
Alex: One reminder: nothing here is financial advice.

137
00:06:57,974 --> 00:06:59,966
Alex: From Bits and Tickers, I'm Alex.

138
00:06:59,966 --> 00:07:00,837
Alex: Keep building.

139
00:07:01,567 --> 00:07:02,135
Maya: I'm Maya.

140
00:07:02,135 --> 00:07:03,270
Maya: And keep counting.
